  "core_idea": "Working memory can hold only a few new elements at once, while long-term memory stores schemas without limit. Instruction works when it avoids load that does not serve learning and helps learners build schemas.",
  "key_concepts_note": "Use the concepts already stored for this theory in the library (theories.concepts).",
  "when_to_use": [
    "Does a format (worked examples, multimedia, split attention) help or hinder learning?",
    "How should complex material be sequenced for novices?",
    "Typical data: experiments with learning tests, subjective load ratings, eye-tracking."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Using the three-part split (intrinsic, extraneous, germane) as if germane load were settled; Sweller later reworked it.",
    "Measuring load with one self-report item and treating it as precise.",
    "Ignoring expertise: what helps novices can hurt experts (expertise reversal)."
  ],
